codex
Methods A and B are sound for the supplied inputs over \(\mathbb F_{32009}\). Exact replays reproduce the reported algebraic counts. The material problems concern completion claims and the interpretation of the E8-bar comparison.

1. **SERIOUS — degree-three agreement does not certify the whole fiber.** The W8 result says \(V(K_2)\) remains an upper bound “until degree 3 or method B closes it” ([decision-record.md:97](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:97)). Agreement at degree three establishes only \(K_3=(K_2)_3\); a new relation can first appear at a higher degree. The record correctly rejects the proposed regularity shortcut elsewhere ([decision-record.md:24](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:24)). Apply the existing finite-segment qualification consistently: X and Iso have no additional cubic relations, while their full fiber ideals remain unresolved ([decision-record.md:79](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:79), [decision-record.md:83](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:83)).

2. **SERIOUS — rank 41 establishes different constant spans, rather than different local ideals or fiber algebras.** I reproduced ranks \(21,21,41\) from [e8_A.txt:1](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/s0b/e8_A.txt:1), [e8_B.txt:1](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/s0b/e8_B.txt:1), and [e8_AB.txt:1](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/s0b/e8_AB.txt:1). The producer reduces both lists modulo \(J\), without quotienting by the maximal ideal times their generated ideal ([e8span.sing:8](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/s0b/e8span.sing:8)). Their constant spans therefore intersect in dimension one, as reported. Polynomial coefficients and local units permit further changes of generators: \(x\) and \(x+x^2\), for example, have independent constant spans but generate the same ideal at the origin. Replace “E8-bar N− is distinct” with “the tested diagonal-sign transport fails”; test localized ideal equivalence before concluding that an independent computation is necessary ([decision-record.md:140](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:140)). Even distinct ideals would require further reasoning to establish inequivalent fiber algebras.

3. **SLOPPY — method B is a completion route, rather than the only completion certificate.** The exclusivity claims exceed the consultant’s recommendation ([decision-record.md:23](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:23), [decision-record.md:119](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:119), [codex-consult-v2.md:474](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/codex-consult-v2.md:474)). Two alternatives deserve bounded investigation:

   - For **full Rees equality**, construct a candidate \(L\) and certify
     \[
     H\subseteq L\subseteq\ker(y_j\mapsto G_j a),\qquad L:g=L.
     \]
     These conditions force \(L=H:g^\infty\). Exact substitution verifies the second inclusion. A fuller presentation from the linear syzygies may make the colon check cheaper; the installed Macaulay2 implementation uses the symmetric-algebra presentation before saturation ([ReesAlgebra.m2:149](/usr/share/Macaulay2/ReesAlgebra.m2:149)). Fiber quadrics require correct Rees lifts before entering \(L\).
   - For **reduced support equality**, X has an irreducible arithmetic quadratic support of projective dimension three and degree twelve ([pC_xm_reduce.log:1](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pC_xm_reduce.log:1)). A certified three-dimensional family of actual limits dense in that support would establish support equality. That certificate would leave scheme structure and the full Hilbert series unresolved ([codex-consult-v2.md:141](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/codex-consult-v2.md:141)).

4. **The second-prime step needs an explicit input-regeneration procedure.** Method B hard-codes characteristic 32009 in both constructed rings ([b_body.sing:10](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/templates/b_body.sing:10), [b_body.sing:17](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/templates/b_body.sing:17)). The producer specializes Gaussian coefficients using the chosen square root of \(-1\) ([nullity16_rees_fibers.py:376](~/worktrees/IBMQuantum/a5-kernel-attachments/docs/evidence/a5-kernel-attachments/nullity16_rees_fibers.py:376)). Regenerate \(J,G\) from the characteristic-zero source at the new split prime and update every ring declaration. Reinterpreting the printed modular coefficients at another prime changes the input. Agreement at two primes still requires the specialization argument acknowledged by the record ([decision-record.md:13](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:13)).

The numerical audit found the following values supported. “Quadratic scheme” means the quotient by the computed quadratic relations, with higher fiber relations still possible.

| Pair | Verified fiber values | Quadratic-scheme projective dimension; degree | Quadratic-scheme Hilbert values |
|---|---|---|---|
| X− | \(15,51,122\) | \(3;\ 12\) | \(15,51,122,240,417\) |
| Iso | \(17,85,265\) | \(4;\ 23\) | \(17,85,265,640\) |
| E8-bar → N+ | \(21,83\) | \(4;\ 11\) | \(21,83,225,495\) |
| W8 → N− | \(24,156\) | \(5;\ 50\) | \(24,156,600,1725\) |

The supporting files are the X [quadratic log:3](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/kaggle-pack-a/xm_nm/singular.log:3), [cubic normal forms:1](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/s1/s1_nf3.txt:1), and [quadratic Hilbert log:9](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pA_xm_rel.log:9); the Iso [quadratic log:1](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pD_iso.log:1) and [cubic normal forms:1](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/s3/s3_nf3.txt:1); the E8 [linear normal forms:1](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/e8_a2/nf1.txt:1), [quadratic normal forms:1](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/e8_a2/nf2.txt:1), and [Hilbert script:1](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/e8_a2/k2hf.sing:1); and the corresponding W8 [linear forms:1](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/w8_a2/nf1.txt:1), [quadratic forms:1](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/w8_a2/nf2.txt:1), and [Hilbert script:1](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/w8_a2/k2hf.sing:1).

**Method A passes the code-level checks.** The quadratic template constructs \(J+\mathfrak mP_d\) and reduces products modulo \(J\) ([a2_body.sing:3](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/templates/a2_body.sing:3)). The cubic code uses actual `kbase` monomials, so `leadexp` retains their entire exponent information; multiplication follows those exponents and reduces after every factor ([s1/input.sing:12](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/s1/input.sing:12)). Certified previous relations give local generation by Nakayama. The resulting product quotient is annihilated by \(\mathfrak m\), making global membership sufficient.

The retained normal-form files use the signed integer coefficients, explicit multiplication, and powers that the parsers handle ([rank_nf.py:13](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/rank_nf.py:13), [relations_nf.py:16](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/relations_nf.py:16)). Independent coefficient-matrix checks confirmed complete relation spaces of dimensions **69, 148, and 144** for X, E8, and W8. The transpose and nullspace-column orientation correctly compute the left kernel ([relations_nf.py:40](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/relations_nf.py:40)).

**Method B also passes.** The colon test establishes \((J:g)=J\), a sufficient global nonzerodivisor condition. The template then forms \(H\), saturates by \(g\), and specializes every source variable to zero ([b_body.sing:5](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/templates/b_body.sing:5), [b_body.sing:11](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/templates/b_body.sing:11)). Rees formation commutes with localization at the origin. After specialization, every inverted source polynomial with nonzero constant term becomes an invertible scalar. Components confined away from the origin therefore contribute nothing to this fiber. The installed `sat` returns an ideal, matching the assignment ([elim.lib:717](/usr/share/singular/LIB/elim.lib:717)).

The symmetry conclusions also pass exact replay:

- X has the recorded signs \(\epsilon=(+,-,-,-,-,-,-,-,+,+,+,+)\) and \(\delta=(+,+,+,+,+,+,-,-,-,-,-,-,-,-,-)\), with zero failures ([s0.log:1](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/s0/s0.log:1)).
- W8 has \(J\) sign \(+\), followed by 22 negative and two positive generator signs, with zero failures ([w8.sing:6](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/s0b/w8.sing:6)). Its script reverses the `p`/`m` labels relative to the named input files, but the involutive substitution gives the same transport.
- E8’s tested substitution matches \(J\) up to sign and fails all 21 generator sign comparisons ([e8.sing:6](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/s0b/e8.sing:6)). Finding 2 states the resulting evidential boundary.

Several smaller claims need narrower wording. Iso’s **23 is the quadratic scheme’s degree**, rather than a relation degree ([purpose.md:17](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/purpose.md:17), [pD_iso.log:1](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pD_iso.log:1)). The reported “std seconds” are cumulative elapsed times because the timer starts before product construction ([a2_body.sing:2](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/templates/a2_body.sing:2)). The X timeout has exit 124 recorded, while its cited log records pivot selection rather than the claimed 6.9G peak ([try3h/rc:1](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/s2/try3h/rc:1), [try3h/out.log:21](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/s2/try3h/out.log:21)). Finally, GTZ coordinate-change densification remains an inferred cause, although the summary presents it as established ([first-principles-plan.md:22](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/first-principles-plan.md:22), [decision-record.md:111](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:111)).

My ranking for the remaining work is an expected-value judgment; comparative completion costs remain unmeasured:

1. **Test localized E8 generator equivalence and pursue an X completion certificate.** The former may eliminate duplicate work; the latter builds on the inexpensive known X support. Investigate the colon/symmetric-algebra or dense-family certificates before another unchanged saturation extension.
2. **Give Iso method B a bounded completion attempt.** Its cubic piece is already certified; repeating the stopped local-syzygy calculation adds little ([decision-record.md:83](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:83)).
3. **Compute E8-bar N− degree two if the equivalence test leaves the row independent.** Use that result to validate method B before assigning more full-saturation time ([decision-record.md:145](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:145)).
4. **Extend E8-bar degree three before W8 degree three when a further finite piece answers a concrete question.** Their candidate counts are 225 and 600, but observed product-building times caution against predicting runtime from counts alone ([e8_a3/out.log:1](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/e8_a3/out.log:1), [w8_a3/out.log:1](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/w8_a3/out.log:1)).
5. **Keep VM 2’s E8-then-W8 saturation experiments bounded and sequential.** Their value is full completion, with the completed method-A pieces serving as cross-checks ([vm2-fiber-b.md:22](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/azure-cases/vm2-fiber-b.md:22)).
6. **Replay selected certificates at the second prime after repairing input generation.** Complete that check before any characteristic-zero conclusion ([decision-record.md:60](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:60)).

The strongest defensible result is a substantial collection of exact modular graded pieces, quadratic upper schemes, and two verified symmetry transports. Full fiber completion remains open, and identifying the algebraic coordinate fiber with the complete real kernel attachment remains a separate obligation ([codex-consult-v2.md:474](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/codex-consult-v2.md:474)).


